<details class="debrief">
  <summary>What this exercise is showing</summary>

  <p>Every piece of information a sighted visitor needs is on that page. Almost none of it is available in text:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The phone number is inside a graphic</strong>, with no alt text. It is not hard to find, it is <em>not there</em>. No amount of skill or patience recovers it.</li>
    <li><strong>The product photographs have filenames for alt text</strong>, so the only difference between the two coats &mdash; one is waterproof &mdash; never reaches the listener.</li>
    <li><strong>Every link says &ldquo;Read more&rdquo; or &ldquo;Click here&rdquo;</strong>, which is fine in a sentence and useless in a links list.</li>
    <li><strong>There are no headings</strong>, so there is no way to jump. You listen to all of it or none of it.</li>
    <li><strong>The basket is an icon button with no name</strong>, announced as &ldquo;button&rdquo;.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The fixed version changes no layout and no visual design at all. It is the same page with alt text, real link text, a heading structure and a phone number that is text and a <code>tel:</code> link. That is usually the honest answer to &ldquo;how much work is this?&rdquo;</p>

  <h3>Questions for the group</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Who got the phone number? What did it feel like to realise it was unreachable rather than hidden?</li>
    <li>What is in our product as an image of text &mdash; prices, offers, infographics, quotes, a slide deck exported as PNGs?</li>
    <li>Look at our own links list. How many say &ldquo;more&rdquo;, &ldquo;here&rdquo;, &ldquo;this page&rdquo; or nothing at all?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What good looks like</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Alt text describes the purpose of the image in that place, not the file. Decorative images take an empty <code>alt=""</code> so they are skipped.</li>
    <li>Never put information in an image of text. If it must be an image, the alt text carries the words.</li>
    <li>Link text makes sense read on its own, out of order and out of context.</li>
    <li>Headings describe the structure honestly, in order, with one <code>h1</code>.</li>
    <li>Icon-only buttons get an accessible name, and it matches any visible tooltip.</li>
  </ul>
</details>
